For the smallest punishments, the whip and stick were used.13 13A similar phrase is found in Han shu 23/2a; Hulsewé, Han Law, 1,322, and Sui shu 25/1b;
     Balazs, Traité juridique, p. 29. See in particular Hulsewé's and Balazs'
     notes on these passages.

Unrighteousness means to kill one's department head, prefect, or magistrate, or
     the teacher from whom one has received one's education.181181The penalty for killing any of these persons is decapitation. See Article 252;
     Deloustal 417, and Article 333; Deloustal 487.

If one behaves like the birds and beasts,191 191This refers to the passage in the Li chi, "Ch'ü li";
     Couvreur, Li i, I, 7, where it is stated that animals have no morality; the stag and his male
     offspring both couplé with the same doe.

and written down in the emperor's archives.201201These last two phrases are paraphrases of Shu ching, "Chun ya ";
     Legge, The Chinese Classics, III, 578-579, and Tso chuan, "Hsi 5"; Legge, The
     Chinese Classics, v, 145.

The achievements of such are recorded on a grand banner.204204This reference is to two people whose merits in the service of the rulers of the Chou
     dynasty were recorded on such a grand banner. See Shu ching, "Chüan ya";
     Legge, The Chinese Classics, In, 578-579.

refers to those punished by life exile or more for involvement in plotting rebellion or
     great sedition.112112These two crimes are the first two of the ten abominations. See Articles 6 and 248;
     Deloustal 410.

Where the offense is punished by penal servitude or life exile [7b] and redemption by
     payment of copper is not allowed, so that the criminal is really sent into life exile or penal
     servitude;

which private households are required not to possess.48 48The penalties for possession of these seals ranged from life exile to decapitation,
     depending upon their being imperial or official, stolen or forged. See Articles 362-364;
     Deloustal 514-516.

This refers to: 1) treating the children of the proper wife as those of a concubine and
     those of a concubine as the children of the proper wife, or violating the law in adoption of
     children;

COMMENTARY: This refers to: 1) treating the children of the proper wife as those of a
     concubine and those of a concubine as the children of the proper wife, or violating the law in
     adoption of children;

COMMENTARY: 2) unauthorized ordainment as a Buddhist or Taoist priest or nun; or
     fraudulent avoidance of taxes and labor services, or leaving one's basic
    status;

None of these will be punished.6161 This reference comes from the Tso chu a n, "Hsiang 29," p. 673;
     Legge, The Chinese Classics, v, 550. It concerns the visit of Kung-tzu Cha of the state of Wu
     to the famous Tzu Ch'an of Cheng in 544 B.C. and their exchange of gifts.

